Doc vers 9007-2
Last Revised 10/10/91



              Meta-Software Graph Data File format

The HSPICE graph data file is created by inserting the .OPTION post, 
post=1, or post=2 into the Hspice input file. It is read by the post-
processors Hsplot and GSI in order to display simulation results 
graphically. 

The Graph data file is a buffered I/O  file.   It  has  a 
physical  structure  and a logical structure.  The  logical  structure 
relates to the information (circuit nodes voltages etc.  and types  of 
analyses performed) contained in the file.  The physical structure  is 
the way the data is stored in the file.

_____________________________________________________________________

FILE NAMING CONVENTIONS

Hspice names the Graph Data File by appending a suffix onto the
current root name (the designname). The suffixes are:
	.tr-	transient analysis results
	.ac-    ac analysis results
 	.dc-	dc analysis results

The "-" is the suffix 0, for a single sweep/run.
It is incremented 0-9,a-z for multiple sweeps/runs which are not placed
in a single file such as "alters". If AAA is the design name, and you
have done 3 transient analyses with two alters,  the files would be 
named: AAA.tr0, AAA.tr1, AAA.tr2.

_____________________________________________________________________

LOGICAL STRUCTURE

The information about a circuit simulation has 2 aspects

1.   Data  Descriptor/Header:   The names, number  and  types  of  the 
signals (voltages currents noise etc), and name of the simulation.

2.  The signal data:  The data produced by the simulation.


                     Data Descriptor/Header

VARIABLE    VARIABLE NAMES                      description
GROUP #     USED IN READRA

1.  16 chars   
                 The following 3 numbers are packed in 16
                 characters in I4 format. Read in a 16 character
                 string, then remap them in FORTRAN as follows: 
                 READ (TEMPCHAR, '(314)') NODCNT, NUMNOI, CNDCNT.

        NODCNT   Total number of nodes where the data are standard 
                 node voltages or branch currents.

        NUMNOI   Total number of special data items. These may be
                 the nodes such as noise data or distortion or
                 fourier or print/plot items.

        CNDCNT   Number of conditions in CONDITIONAL record below.
                 As of 9007 only 1 possible cndcnt can exist, that 
                 the file contains multiple sweeps.
                 cndcnt = O means this file has no multiple sweeps.
                 cndcnt = 1 means there are multiple sweeps and
                 SWEEPCNT below contains the number of sweeps.

2.   4 chars
        VERSION  Version id used to distinguish which version of
                 the format generated by HSPICE. 
                     8807--1st valid available version
                     9001--uses 1st 4 MONTIC chars for sweepcount.
		     9007--h9007b and successors delete graph data 
                           file instead of overwriting file. Allows
                           distinguishing between rerun and concurrent
                           run. No other change in graph data file.
     4 chars
        UNUSED

    72 chars
        QTITLE   TITLE card header title from circuit

     8 chars
        CDATE    Date circuit was run.  

     8 chars   
        CTIME    Time circuit was run.  

3.  72 chars
        TITL2    COPYWRITE line and company site designator.

4.   4 chars
        SWEEPCNT Number of sweeps in this file. (added in vers 9001)

    76 chars
	MONTIC   Reserved for future use with monticarlo
                 runs. (was 80 chars previous to ver 9001)
    8 chars
5.      ITYP (I) integer formated as I3 in an  8 character field.
                 Item types correspond to each name in the CNAM
                 list below. 
                 For a complete listing of the meaning of the
                 type number see the last section below on Data Types.

   16 chars
6.      CNAM(I)  Character names for each data item in the data
                 record.  Each name is 16 bytes long. Names are 
                 ordered: 
                 	Independent Variable
                        Node voltages and branch currents
                        Special items (print/plot, noise etc.)
                        Sweep variable name (sweeps only) 

   16 chars
7.      END_OF_HEADER  $&%#
                 Signifies end of header information. Any characters
                 that follow this symbol up to the end of the header
                 block are garbage. Ignore them.

                         The simulation DATA

The logical structure of the data values is a sequence of records, one 
for each value of the independent variable (time, freq or parameter).
The logical records have the following structure.

    4 bytes/number
1.      INDVBL   The value of the independent variable for this
                 record.

    4 bytes/number  (ac analysis: 8 bytes for 2 numbers)
2.      VALUES(NODCNT) Voltages and Current.  Single precision real or
                 complex data.  This is NODCNT  in length
                 These are the voltage and current values for each
                 signal in the order they appear in CNAM (ITEM 6
                 ABOVE).  
                 
                 For ac analysis this data is Complex
                 data therefore single precision numbers must be read for
                 each name in CNAM, the first is the real, the second
                 is the imaginary value.  Voltages for all nodes are
                 included. Currents are included only for sources.  
                 Since a dummy source is
                 created whenever the current through an element is
                 printed/plotted in the hspice run, the currents are
                 included for all such elements.

    4 bytes/number
3.       NOISE + PRTPLT items
3a.      NOISE(NUMNOI) The NOISE data: (For ac analysis only.)  
                 This  data is not complex.  
                 The names for these items are in the
                 corresponding locations in CNAM. 

3b.      PRTPLT(NUMNOI) The PRINT/PLOT items: (ac, dc and tran) All
                 items which appeared in a print or plot statement  in 
                 the hspice run are appended to the record.  The names
                 for these items are in CNAM.  Signals may appear more
                 than  once  in this data.  They will appear  here  as  
                 many times as they appear in print or plot statements
                 in the hspice run.  There will be NUMNOI items.

    4 bytes/number
5.       ENDSWP: Every run and/or sweep is terminated by the real datum: 
                 0.10000E+31.	

			Sweep Data

If the file contains multiple sweeps and the sweeps are generated
by an outer loop variable, the value of that variable for any
particular sweep is written before any other data for the sweep.
So for a file containing multiple sweeps, the logical view looks like:
		Header (node names/types etc.)
sweep 1 start:  Outer Sweep value
        	INDVBL
        	VALUES
        	NOISE and/or PRTPLT
sweep 1 end:    0.10000E+31
sweep 2 start:  Outer Sweep value
        	INDVBL
        	VALUES
        	NOISE and/or PRTPLT
sweep 2 end:    0.10000E+31
                   .
                   .
                   .
                   .
sweep n start:  Outer Sweep value
        	INDVBL
        	VALUES
        	NOISE and/or PRTPLT
sweep n end:    0.10000E+31
                  
_____________________________________________________________________

PHYSICAL STRUCTURE

HSPICE  provides  two physical formats for the graphic  data  file,  a 
binary format and an ascii or character formatted file. The  character 
format is used when hspice is run on a different machine than hsplot
or gsi is run on.  The character formatted graph data file can be  
transfered to a machine with graphic capabilities and GSI or HSPLOT 
can be run  on  the ascii  files.  

The binary file is used when Hspice and Hsplot/GSI 
are run on the same machine. The  binary 
file  can  be read and written up to 10 times faster  than  the  ascii 
format.   The  ascii  format  is selected by  setting  post=2  in  the 
".options"  statement in  the  spice input file.   Post or post=1 
selects the binary format .                 

Be  aware that these files are generated by fortran unformatted  writes. 
The  internal  structure may not be compatable with reads  from  other 
languages  unless the machine dependant fields in the binary file  are 
known.  
               
Both  formats  have two sections, the header character  data  and  the 
signal real data.


                          The BINARY format

The  binary  is implemented as a machine independent  variable  length 
record  unformatted  fortran file.  A binary record may be from  1  to 
8192 bytes in length. Each header record (block) is preceded by a 4 
byte integer representing the number of 8 byte groups in the
record which follows. Each signal data record (block) is preceded by a 4 
byte integer representing the number of real numbers (4 bytes)
in the record which follows. 

This 4 byte integer in both cases is "wrapped" by most Fortran
writes as follows:
  a.	4 bytes		#bytes containing following size integer
  b.	4 bytes		the "size" integer described above
  c.	4 bytes		#bytes containing following size integer
  d.    4 bytes         the size of following record in bytes

Typical example of a size record as displayed by the unix octal dump
(od -d):
0000000  00000 00004 00000 00082 00000 00004 00000 00656
                   a           b           c           d

where each string of 5 digits above represents 2 bytes

At the end of each block, fortran writes a trailer, a 4 byte integer 
which is the size of the block in bytes. In other words, it writes 
the same number as the "d" integer, above.

The  first records in the file are the header block(s).  These  blocks 
contain  unformatted  character data.  The first record is  written as 
a 4 byte integer representing the number of 8 byte groups of characters
in  the record  which  follows. To read the next record read  8  times
this number of characters from the file. All subsequent blocks will be 
preceeded by an integer size record, described above. Header blocks 
should be buffered until the terminator $&%# is read indicating that 
there is no more header data, then interpret it according to the logical 
structure above. Header data will not appear in the same record with signal 
data. A new record for the signal data is started when the header is 
done.  The format is like:
***********************************************************************
block size header record (4 - 4 byte integers) (eg: 0004 1024 0004 8192 )
header data block 1
block size trailer (1 - 4 byte integer) (eg: 8192 )
***********************************************************************
block size header record (16 bytes)
header data block 2
block size trailer (4 bytes)
***********************************************************************
etc

***********************************************************************
block size header record (16 bytes)
final header data block....ending with $&%#
block size trailer (4 bytes)
*********************************************

Subsequent  blocks/records in the file are signal data  records.   The 
first record following the header is the size record, described above.
The structure of the file is like:

***********************************************************************
block size header record (4 - 4 byte integers) (eg: 0004 2048 0004 8192 )
signal data block 1
block size trailer (1 - 4 byte integer) (eg: 8192 )
***********************************************************************
block size header record (16 bytes)
signal data block 2
block size trailer (4 bytes)
***********************************************************************
etc

***********************************************************************
block size header record (16 bytes)
final signal block of 1st sweep or if no sweep end of file also.
....ending with 1e+30
block size trailer (4 bytes)

*********************************************
(Begin of next sweep/trial--if one exists)
(First CNDKNT numbers are the values of the parameter(s) for this sweep 
so you must read the first cndcnt numbers before beginning to  read
the logical records.)
(As of release 9007b, CNDKNT can be at most "1")
block size header record (16 bytes)
signal block 1 of next sweep 
block size trailer (4 bytes)
etc

******************************************************************

